Example 6.1 gives an order isomorphism between the elements of the sharp domain and the elements of the sum of the original system with the product of two copies of the sharp domain. The forward map converts into the sum and the inverse converts back, and the two round-trip lemmas say they are mutual inverses. The forward map preserves and reflects order because a neighbourhood of the smaller sharp element is the master, a zero copy, or a pair copy, and the corresponding sum membership passes to the larger image, while a sum neighbourhood of an image is the master, a left injection, or a right injection, and the same cases pass the membership back.
